1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,922
A cook in Chennai and a cook in Delhi
both weigh one kilogram of rice.

2
00:00:04,922 --> 00:00:07,192
Every watch counts the same second.

3
00:00:07,592 --> 00:00:08,826
They never met.

4
00:00:08,826 --> 00:00:13,531
So how do they agree on exactly
how much a kilogram or a second is?

5
00:00:13,981 --> 00:00:15,950
Aarav finds out the hard way.

6
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:18,588
His spanner is marked
thirteen millimetres.

7
00:00:18,938 --> 00:00:23,144
His bicycle bolt is half an inch,
twelve point seven millimetres.

8
00:00:23,144 --> 00:00:24,458
The spanner slips.

9
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:27,781
A gap of just zero
point three millimetres.

10
00:00:27,781 --> 00:00:31,075
That is why the world
agreed on one set of units.

11
00:00:31,725 --> 00:00:35,291
That set is called SI, the
International System of Units.

12
00:00:35,641 --> 00:00:38,983
It has seven base units,
one for each base quantity.

13
00:00:39,383 --> 00:00:40,682
Metre for length.

14
00:00:40,682 --> 00:00:42,032
Kilogram for mass.

15
00:00:42,032 --> 00:00:43,281
Second for time.

16
00:00:43,281 --> 00:00:45,200
Ampere for electric current.

17
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,240
Kelvin for temperature.

18
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:49,317
Mole for amount of substance.

19
00:00:49,317 --> 00:00:51,435
Candela for luminous intensity.

20
00:00:51,835 --> 00:00:54,621
Every other unit is
built from these seven.

21
00:00:54,621 --> 00:00:56,256
Those are derived units.

22
00:00:56,906 --> 00:00:59,341
But who decides how long one metre is?

23
00:00:59,341 --> 00:01:00,915
Let's watch light do it.

24
00:01:01,265 --> 00:01:05,854
Light in vacuum always travels at the
same speed: two hundred ninety nine

25
00:01:05,854 --> 00:01:10,523
million, seven hundred ninety two
thousand, four hundred fifty eight metres

26
00:01:10,523 --> 00:01:11,444
per second.

27
00:01:11,844 --> 00:01:16,529
So one metre is the distance light
travels in that tiny fraction of a second:

28
00:01:16,529 --> 00:01:18,546
one part in this exact number.

29
00:01:18,946 --> 00:01:21,086
Watch the pulse cross one metre.

30
00:01:21,086 --> 00:01:24,611
The clock reads about three
point three four nanoseconds.

31
00:01:25,011 --> 00:01:25,915
Slow motion.

32
00:01:25,915 --> 00:01:28,318
The blue arrow is the speed of light.

33
00:01:28,318 --> 00:01:31,531
It never changes, so the
graph is a straight line.

34
00:01:31,931 --> 00:01:33,117
Its slope is c.

35
00:01:33,117 --> 00:01:35,412
Same light, same metre, anywhere.

36
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:39,967
The other base units are tied
to fixed numbers of nature too.

37
00:01:40,317 --> 00:01:44,791
The second comes from the caesium
atom: nine billion, one hundred ninety

38
00:01:44,791 --> 00:01:49,500
two million, six hundred thirty one
thousand, seven hundred seventy ticks.

39
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,708
The kilogram is fixed
by the Planck constant.

40
00:01:52,708 --> 00:01:56,335
Until twenty nineteen, it was
a metal cylinder near Paris.

41
00:01:56,735 --> 00:02:00,482
Nature's numbers never change,
so the units never drift.

42
00:02:01,132 --> 00:02:04,048
Now let's build a derived
unit step by step.

43
00:02:04,048 --> 00:02:06,806
The joule, the unit of work and energy.

44
00:02:07,156 --> 00:02:08,080
Step one.

45
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:10,785
Force equals mass times acceleration.

46
00:02:10,785 --> 00:02:14,842
So one newton is one kilogram
metre per second squared.

47
00:02:15,592 --> 00:02:16,492
Step two.

48
00:02:16,459 --> 00:02:18,787
Work equals force times distance.

49
00:02:18,787 --> 00:02:21,897
So one joule is one
newton times one metre.

50
00:02:22,647 --> 00:02:23,600
Step three.

51
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:25,041
Put the newton in.

52
00:02:25,041 --> 00:02:28,980
One joule is one kilogram metre
squared per second squared.

53
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:32,051
Every named unit hides base units.

54
00:02:32,051 --> 00:02:34,633
The pascal is newton per square metre.

55
00:02:34,633 --> 00:02:36,703
The watt is joule per second.

56
00:02:37,453 --> 00:02:38,507
Remember this.

57
00:02:38,507 --> 00:02:39,767
Seven base units.

58
00:02:39,767 --> 00:02:42,198
Every other unit is built from them.

59
00:02:42,898 --> 00:02:44,076
Side by side.

60
00:02:44,076 --> 00:02:46,269
The kilogram is a base unit.

61
00:02:46,269 --> 00:02:47,830
Chosen, not built.

62
00:02:48,230 --> 00:02:49,772
The newton is derived.

63
00:02:49,772 --> 00:02:54,116
It has its own name, but inside
it is kilogram, metre and second.

64
00:02:54,716 --> 00:02:55,801
Now you try.

65
00:02:55,801 --> 00:02:59,592
The watt is named after a
scientist, like the newton.

66
00:02:59,592 --> 00:03:01,540
Base unit or derived unit?

67
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:02,888
Pause and decide.

68
00:03:06,088 --> 00:03:06,988
Derived!

69
00:03:06,814 --> 00:03:09,084
A watt is one joule per second.

70
00:03:09,084 --> 00:03:13,235
Open it, and you get kilogram
metre squared per second cubed.

71
00:03:13,885 --> 00:03:16,600
SI also has rules for writing units.

72
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:17,952
Exams love them.

73
00:03:18,302 --> 00:03:22,851
Unit names are written in small
letters, even when named after a person.

74
00:03:22,851 --> 00:03:24,939
The unit newton has a small n.

75
00:03:25,339 --> 00:03:28,321
But the symbol of a
person's unit is a capital.

76
00:03:28,321 --> 00:03:31,247
N for newton, Pa for pascal, K for kelvin.

77
00:03:31,647 --> 00:03:34,596
Other symbols stay small: m, s, kg.

78
00:03:34,996 --> 00:03:37,500
No plural, and no full stop.

79
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:41,368
Five kilograms is five
k g, never five K g s.

80
00:03:42,018 --> 00:03:42,997
Your turn.

81
00:03:42,997 --> 00:03:45,438
A label says five capital K g.

82
00:03:45,438 --> 00:03:46,822
Is that correct?

83
00:03:46,822 --> 00:03:48,262
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:51,462 --> 00:03:52,362
No!

85
00:03:51,949 --> 00:03:54,831
Capital K is the kelvin, a temperature.

86
00:03:54,831 --> 00:03:57,396
The kilogram is small k, small g.

87
00:03:58,046 --> 00:03:59,781
Now, one worked example.

88
00:04:00,131 --> 00:04:04,122
Aarav pushes his bicycle with
twenty newtons for fifteen metres.

89
00:04:04,122 --> 00:04:06,408
Find the work done in base units.

90
00:04:06,908 --> 00:04:11,422
Work equals force times distance:
twenty newtons times fifteen metres.

91
00:04:12,172 --> 00:04:15,819
That is three hundred newton
metres, or three hundred joules.

92
00:04:16,569 --> 00:04:20,500
Now open the joule: kilogram
metre squared per second squared.

93
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:25,541
So the work is three hundred kilogram
metre squared per second squared.

94
00:04:26,191 --> 00:04:27,657
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:28,007 --> 00:04:32,585
Many students say the gram is the SI
base unit of mass, because kilo is

96
00:04:32,585 --> 00:04:33,710
just a prefix.

97
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:36,986
But the SI base unit of
mass is the kilogram.

98
00:04:36,986 --> 00:04:40,308
It is the only base unit
with a prefix in its name.

99
00:04:40,958 --> 00:04:42,322
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:42,322 --> 00:04:46,763
As one mark questions: a unit in base
units, the base unit, or the wrong

101
00:04:46,763 --> 00:04:47,663
symbol.

102
00:04:47,967 --> 00:04:49,174
Exam level now.

103
00:04:49,174 --> 00:04:53,583
Sunlight intensity is measured in
joule per second per square metre.

104
00:04:53,583 --> 00:04:55,803
What is this in SI base units?

105
00:04:55,803 --> 00:04:57,061
Pause and solve.

106
00:05:02,461 --> 00:05:03,866
The answer is A.

107
00:05:03,866 --> 00:05:05,814
Kilogram per second cubed.

108
00:05:06,314 --> 00:05:10,608
Joule per second is the watt: kilogram
metre squared per second cubed.

109
00:05:10,608 --> 00:05:12,844
That is B, but we are not done.

110
00:05:13,344 --> 00:05:14,940
Divide by square metre.

111
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:18,327
The metre squared cancels:
kilogram per second cubed.

112
00:05:18,327 --> 00:05:21,345
C is the pascal, and
D keeps a stray metre.

113
00:05:21,945 --> 00:05:23,406
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:23,406 --> 00:05:24,907
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:28,107 --> 00:05:30,944
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:31,344 --> 00:05:32,244
One.

117
00:05:31,869 --> 00:05:34,096
How is the metre defined today?

118
00:05:34,996 --> 00:05:35,896
Two.

119
00:05:35,554 --> 00:05:38,365
Write the joule using only base units.

120
00:05:39,265 --> 00:05:40,165
Three.

121
00:05:39,924 --> 00:05:41,774
Why is five K g wrong?

122
00:05:42,874 --> 00:05:46,290
Next time: the tyre says
psi, the pump says bar.

123
00:05:46,290 --> 00:05:48,835
Why do other unit systems still exist?

124
00:05:48,835 --> 00:05:51,083
We find out in systems of units.
